  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jack Perkins shut them down and the offense still couldn't find a run that mattered.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what happens when the pitching holds and the bats refuse to cooperate. Perkins threw six and two-thirds scoreless innings. Eight strikeouts, four hits, zero runs. And the Athletics still lost 2-1. The bullpen stayed clean after him. Luis Medina and Hogan Harris combined for one and a third scoreless. But in the third inning with a runner in scoring position, they got nothing. Empty at-bat. The rest of the game looked the same. Four hits total. Twelve strikeouts. The runs came from Tampa Bay in the second and fifth, one apiece, and the A's never answered. This is the inconsistency that defines the season. Pitching good enough to win, offense not good enough to do it. The gap between what the team can keep tight and what it can actually close keeps getting wider. One run in six and two-thirds innings of shutdown baseball is not a loss you survive.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tampa Bay's Nick Martinez arrives with a 2.92 ERA and the A's need anything close to what Perkins delivered today to get a different result.",
